Make the ORDER BY match the index exactly. For your index on (user_id, ts), something like:

SELECT DISTINCT ON (user_id) * FROM events ORDER BY user_id, ts DESC;

Postgres can scan the index backwards to satisfy ts DESC, so you don't need a separate sort node. Then run EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS) on it. You want to see an Index Scan or Index Only Scan with no Sort above it. If you see a Sort, check that the column order in the index matches the ORDER BY and that the table has been analyzed recently, since stale stats can make the planner pick a sequential scan and sort instead.
